  "answer_text": "Absolutely \u2014 what we eat matters enormously! Here's a comprehensive overview of why:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf4e Yes, What We Eat Matters \u2014 A Lot!\n\n### 1. **Food Is Information for Your Body**\nThe food we eat gives our bodies the \"information\" and materials they need to function properly. If we don't get the right information, our metabolic processes suffer and our health declines. If we get too much food, or food that gives our bodies the wrong instructions, we can become overweight, undernourished, and at risk for diseases such as arthritis, diabetes, and heart disease.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Diet and Disease**\nDiet plays a critical role in shaping the health and well-being of both individuals and populations, and unhealthy diets are a major risk factor for disease and disability.\n\nFor decades, it has been known that the occurrence and course of a variety of typical Western diseases \u2014 such as cancer, stroke, and myocardial infarction \u2014 are affected by diet.\n\nRecent research shows that a diet rich in red meat and processed meat leads to a high risk of cancer, especially colorectal cancer. Substantial evidence indicates that eating more vegetables and fruits and less meat is able to decrease overall cancer risk and improve health.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Quality Matters, Not Just Calories**\nTwo new diet studies add to the evidence that when it comes to staying healthy, counting calories may not be enough. What you choose to eat really does matter.\n\nTrendy diets and nutrition research change almost daily. Still, amidst all the hype, study after study shows that good food choices have a positive impact on health, and poor diets have negative long-term effects.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Mental Health Is Affected Too**\nEating a poor diet reduces physical and mental health because eating healthy allows people to be more active. Two-thirds of people who eat fresh fruit and vegetables daily report no mental health issues. Compare this to those who do have some level of mental health problems \u2014 they have generally reported eating less healthy foods and more unhealthy foods.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **The Role of Processed Foods**\nPeople are now consuming more highly processed foods high in unhealthy fats, free sugars and salt/sodium, and many people do not eat enough fruit and vegetables or consume sufficient dietary fibre.\n\nThe Standard American Diet relies heavily on processed foods that include artificial color, additives, flavorings, and chemically-altered fats and sweeteners. These additives and chemically altered substances may be giving our bodies the wrong signals, instead of the information they need to function properly.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **The Economic Impact**\nAccording to the U.S. Department of Agriculture, a healthier diet could prevent at least $71 billion per year in medical costs and lost lives \u2014 and that number may be underestimated because it only accounts for diet-related coronary heart disease, stroke, cancer, and diabetes.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Diet Is Personal**\nThe exact make-up of a diversified, balanced and healthy diet will vary depending on individual characteristics (e.g., age, gender, lifestyle and degree of physical activity), cultural context, locally-available foods and dietary customs.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 The Bottom Line\nWhen we talk about healthy eating, we mean eating a wide variety of foods in the right proportions. Following a healthy, balanced diet helps your body to get the nutrition and energy it needs to stay healthy, active and well \u2014 maintaining good health, a healthy weight, and helping you to feel your best.\n\nSo yes \u2014 what you eat profoundly shapes your physical health, mental health, energy levels, and long-term risk of disease. It truly is one of the most powerful tools you have for living well!",
